Decode a cached texture again when its bytes change

Fast3D keeps each texture it decodes in a cache, under the address of its texels, the addresses of its palette, its
format and its size. A later draw of the same texture takes the decoded picture from there. Nothing in the key says
what the bytes were, so when a game changes a texture where it is, or frees the memory and loads another texture at
the same address, Fast3D goes on drawing the old picture.

WCW/nWo Revenge does both: it edits textures and palettes in place (the wrestlers' names, palettes turned grey) and
loads new textures into buffers it reuses. A game can ask Fast3D to forget an address (gfx_texture_cache_delete), but
only if it knows every place that writes texture memory.

Each cache entry now also keeps a fingerprint of the bytes the texture was decoded from (Interpreter::TextureContent):
its texels, read as the decoders read them, and for a CI texture the palette entries it uses, from Fast3D's own copy
of the palette. A lookup that finds the entry with another fingerprint decodes the texture again, into the same
texture. N64 textures are at most a few kilobytes, so working out the fingerprint at each use costs far less than
decoding. Replacement textures and textures from resource files cannot change and keep a fingerprint of 0.

